\chapter{Function values and higher-order calls}
\label{ch:11}
\index{Function values and higher-order calls}

A function value has a structural type recording each parameter and the result. A call may target a lexical value, a top-level function, an intrinsic, or a raw extern. The checker resolves which case applies. This matters when a local function value shadows a top-level function. The lowering uses the checked callee, preserving the original lookup.

\OnxInputCode[]{../../on1x/examples/higher_order.on1x}
A higher-order helper can accept an already chosen function and apply it to an ordinary argument. Check argument order and the type of each nested result when composing helpers. A call with the wrong arity fails at type checking, even when a local name happens to have the same spelling as a different function. At the C boundary, internal callable values can carry an environment and a typed invocation pointer. Treat that representation as compiler-owned. Raw extern declarations do not automatically admit function-valued arguments.

\section{Read successive parentheses as successive calls}
In \OnxCodeTerm{select(false)(9)}, the first pair of parentheses belongs
to a function taking a Boolean. Its result is a callable, so the second
pair belongs to that result. The false arm returns the doubling function:
the number printed is 18. By contrast, calling the selected function
with no argument would have the wrong arity, and supplying 9 as a
second argument to the selector would have the wrong arity at an
entirely different call. Check the type immediately to the left of each
opening parenthesis to distinguish the two mistakes.

In the same example \OnxCodeTerm{apply} names the two-argument helper
\OnxCodeTerm{twice}. Its first argument supplies the operation and the
second supplies a starting number. On input 3 with doubling, the inner
application maps 3 to 6, then the outer one maps 6 to 12. Compare that
with invoking the selected doubling function just once. The number of
arguments to the helper and the number of times it invokes its parameter
happen to be the same here, but are independent parts of the program.
As an exercise pass increment instead, keeping the start at 3. The
expected result is 5, with no change to the helper's signature or body.

